meta {
  name: Review Audit
  type: http
  seq: 16
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/deal-audits/deals/{{auditDealId}}/review
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{auditReviewerToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "decision": "request_changes",
    "notes": "The Good Student transcript is from 2019 — please upload this year's."
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("sends the audit back to the producer", function () {
    // `request_changes` is the Deal Audit page's Send back (PAC-106): `Fail`
    // is what the producer's board paints red with a Fix it action.
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.auditStatus).to.equal("Fail");
    expect(body).to.have.property("reviewedAt");
  });

  test("stamps the sent-back marker the producer sees", function () {
    const sentBack = res.getBody().sentBack;
    expect(sentBack).to.be.an("object");
    expect(sentBack.note).to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
    expect(sentBack.failedItemCount).to.be.at.least(1);
    expect(sentBack).to.have.property("byName");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /deal-audits/deals/:dealId/review — the manager's decision (PAC-72, PAC-106)

  Approve or Send back on the Deal Audit page.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits`
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:write` **and** `management:read` — an AND-set
    (PAC-106). Reviewing is the manager's job; the Producer template holds
    `deal_audits:write` and is now refused here.
  - **Data scope:** the audit must be in the caller's reach — branch for a
    branch manager, agency for the owner, assignee/reviewer under `own`.

  Only an audit in **Pending** can be reviewed — anything else is `409`.

  > 🔴 **The submitter cannot review their own audit** — `403`. An audit
  > submitted automatically (`submittedById: null`, nothing for the producer to
  > provide) has no submitter, so any manager may review it.

  ## Decisions
  | decision          | lands on        | rule |
  |-------------------|-----------------|------|
  | `approve`         | `Pass`          | **every item's verdict is `pass`** (PAC-106) — else `409 audit_items_not_passed` |
  | `request_changes` | `Fail`          | **Send back**: at least one item `fail` (else `409 audit_nothing_failed`) **and** a `notes` (else `400`) |
  | `send_back`       | `Not Submitted` | unchanged from PAC-72; not offered by the Deal Audit page |

  Verdicts are set item by item with **Set Item Verdict** first. An item that
  does not apply is passed with a note, not skipped.

  ### What Send back does
  1. Every item with verdict `fail` is **reopened** — `isFailed: true`,
     `isResolved: false`, back to *In Progress* — so it is back on the
     producer's board with the manager's note (`review.note`). A failed
     manager-only item joins the board too (`managerOnly: true`).
  2. Stamps `sentBack: { at, byId, note, failedItemCount }`, which the
     producer's red card and drawer show as "Sent back by … — note".
  3. Writes `audit_changes_requested` to the thread ("Sent back: <note>").
  4. Requests an **`audit.sent_back`** notification (email + push) for the
     audit's assignee — a role assignee notifies every active holder of the
     role — never the manager who sent it. Its link opens the producer's
     drawer: `/dashboard/producer?audit=<dealId>`. Best-effort: a failed
     dispatch is logged and the send-back still succeeds.

  The producer fixes, resubmits (**Submit Audit For Review**), and the failed
  verdicts are cleared for a fresh ruling; the passed ones stand.

  Steps 1–2, the recomputed counters and the mirror on `Deal.dealAuditStatus`
  are **one transaction** (PAC-106 review) — they used to be separate writes,
  and a failure between them could leave a `Fail` audit with no reopened items
  that the producer's board then hid. Steps 3–4 run after it commits.

  ## Body
  | field       | type     | rules |
  |-------------|----------|-------|
  | decision    | enum     | required — `approve` \| `request_changes` \| `send_back` |
  | notes       | string   | ≤ 2000 chars; **required when `request_changes`** — it is the marker text and the notification body |
  | reasonCodes | string[] | optional since PAC-106 (the failed items and the note say what is wrong); ignored on approve |
  | score       | number   | integer 0–100 |

  `reasonCodes` vocabulary (SmartSuite's own): `Missing Docs`,
  `Coverage Not Offered`, `Incorrect Named Insured`, `Incorrect Address`,
  `Underwriting Issue`, `Other`. **Cleared on any non-failure outcome**.

  ## Response `201`
  The full audit — same shape as **Get Audit Workflow**, including `sentBack`.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — unknown decision, or `request_changes` with no `notes`.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing `deal_audits:write` or `management:read`, or the
    caller submitted this audit.
  - `404 Not Found` — no audit for that deal, or out of reach.
  - `409 Conflict` — the audit is not awaiting review (including a second
    approve, or another manager ruling first); or
    ```json
    {
      "statusCode": 409,
      "error": "Conflict",
      "code": "audit_items_not_passed",
      "message": "Every item must pass before the audit can be approved.",
      "unmet": [{ "id": "665f...ac1", "itemName": "Good Student" }]
    }
    ```
    or `code: "audit_nothing_failed"` for a Send back with no failed item.
}
